<img width="350" height="490" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Marinade-for-flank-steak-350x490.jpg" class="webfeedsFeaturedVisual wp-post-image" alt="Marinated flank steak sliced on a plate." style="display: block; margin-bottom: 5px; clear:both;max-width: 100%;" link_thumbnail="" decoding="async" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Marinade-for-flank-steak-350x490.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Marinade-for-flank-steak-768x1075.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Marinade-for-flank-steak-1097x1536.jpg 1097w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Marinade-for-flank-steak-400x560.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Marinade-for-flank-steak-150x210.jpg 150w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Marinade-for-flank-steak.jpg 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 350px) 100vw, 350px" />
<div class="wp-block-group is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p>I&rsquo;ve been making flank steak for years, but it wasn&rsquo;t until we bought our first gas grill a couple of summers ago that I really started perfecting it. I was first drawn to this cut because it&rsquo;s budget-friendly and cooks quickly, which makes it perfect for busy weeknights or casual weekend grilling.</p>



<p>Back then, I kept things simple with just salt and pepper. The steak was fine, but it never had that &ldquo;wow&rdquo; factor. That changed once I began experimenting with marinades, and the version you see here quickly became my go-to.&nbsp;</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="800" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Grilled-flank-steak-marinade.jpg" alt="Grilled flank steak sliced on a plate." class="wp-image-82797" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Grilled-flank-steak-marinade.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Grilled-flank-steak-marinade-350x233.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Grilled-flank-steak-marinade-768x512.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Grilled-flank-steak-marinade-400x267.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Grilled-flank-steak-marinade-150x100.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Grilled-flank-steak-marinade.jpg"></figure>



<p>What makes this the best flank steak marinade is the balance of soy sauce, vinegar, Dijon mustard, and a touch of honey (or brown sugar). Paired with high heat and a quick cook time, it transforms this lean cut into a tender, juicy steak that people can hardly believe is flank steak.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-ingredients-you-ll-need">Ingredients You&rsquo;ll Need</h2>



<p>The full list of ingredients is <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/flank-steak-marinade/#wprm-recipe-container-82806">in the recipe card below</a>, but after making this recipe countless times, I&rsquo;ve gathered a few notes on what makes these key ingredients so important:</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="800" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Flank-Steak-Marinade-Ingredients-1200x800.jpg" alt="Ingredient for the recipe from the top view." class="wp-image-82798" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Flank-Steak-Marinade-Ingredients.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Flank-Steak-Marinade-Ingredients-350x233.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Flank-Steak-Marinade-Ingredients-768x512.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Flank-Steak-Marinade-Ingredients-400x267.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Flank-Steak-Marinade-Ingredients-150x100.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Flank-Steak-Marinade-Ingredients.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Olive oil:</strong> Use a good-quality extra virgin olive oil here. Nowadays, my favorite brand is <a href="https://amzn.to/3Ukcp3A" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener nofollow">Garcia de la Cruz</a> (affiliate link) with its smooth and earthy flavor. You&rsquo;ll also want to keep a little extra oil to grease your grill grates right before grilling to prevent the meat from sticking to the grill.&nbsp;</p>



<p><strong>Soy sauce:</strong> Soy sauce is the secret ingredient that gives the steak its deep, salty, savory, and umami kick. I&rsquo;ve tried using just salt before, but soy sauce adds so much more depth. If you&rsquo;re following a gluten-free diet, tamari or coconut aminos work just as well.</p>



<p><strong>Apple Cider Vinegar:</strong> As the primary acid in this recipe, vinegar helps tenderize the meat. I use apple cider vinegar in this recipe, as it is commonly found in most households. However, balsamic vinegar or red wine vinegar would also work. Keep in mind, though, depending on the vinegar you use, that the flavor profile of your marinade will be slightly different.</p>



<p>For example, using an aged balsamic from Modena will give you a deeper, sweeter taste.</p>



<p><strong>Lemon or lime juice:</strong> I recommend using freshly squeezed juice for the best taste. I usually go with lemon juice for its slightly tangier flavor, but lime juice works just as well. While freshly squeezed juice is best, in a pinch, bottled juice will work too.</p>



<p><strong>Dijon mustard:</strong> When it comes to mustard, I like to use Dijon mustard for its strong, tangy flavors, but if you prefer a bit of texture in your marinade, you can also use grainy mustard. Yellow mustard will work in a pinch, though it has a milder taste.</p>



<p><strong>Sweetener of your choice:</strong> A small amount of sugar helps balance the tangy and umami flavors of the marinade. I use honey in my recipe, but brown sugar would also work.&nbsp; Either way, be sure to whisk it well so that it&rsquo;s fully dissolved to prevent the sugar burning on the grill.</p>



<p><strong>Fresh garlic cloves:</strong> For the best taste, I recommend using freshly minced garlic. But if you prefer a milder flavor, garlic powder works too.</p>



<p><strong>Seasoning for Flank Steak:</strong> For the spices in this marinade, I&rsquo;ve found that sweet paprika and ground cumin work best, but if you prefer a smokier flavor, you can swap in smoked paprika. You can also add fresh or dried herbs that complement the rest of your meal. Choose ones that echo the flavors in your side dishes for a harmonious pairing.</p>



<p><strong>Flank steak:</strong> When buying flank steak, look for a cut that is well marbled and has a bright red color that looks fresh and moist. It is best to choose a flank steak that is even in thickness. My recipe calls for 1 &frac12; lbs of flank steak that is&nbsp; &frac34; to 1 inch thick, which is ideal for grilling.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-how-to-marinate-flank-steak-step-by-step-instructions">How to Marinate Flank Steak? Step-by-Step Instructions</h2>



<p>Marinating flank steak is one of the simplest ways to ensure it turns out tender and full of flavor. It really is as easy as whisking a few ingredients together in a large bowl and letting the steak soak up all that goodness.</p>



<p>Here&rsquo;s exactly how I do it with some helpful tips on every step:</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Best-flank-steak-marinade.jpg" alt="Flank steak marinade in a bowl and being poured over the meat." class="wp-image-82799" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Best-flank-steak-marinade.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Best-flank-steak-marinade-350x217.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Best-flank-steak-marinade-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Best-flank-steak-marinade-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Best-flank-steak-marinade-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Best-flank-steak-marinade.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 1 &ndash; Make the marinade:</strong> In a mixing bowl, combine olive oil, soy sauce, vinegar, lemon juice, Worcestershire sauce, Dijon mustard, honey, garlic, paprika, cumin, salt, and pepper. Whisk until everything is fully incorporated and the marinade looks smooth.&nbsp;</p>



<p>I find it helps to start with the wet ingredients first, then add in the seasonings so everything blends evenly. This is especially helpful when using ingredients like honey or Dijon mustard, as it ensures they dissolve smoothly and don&rsquo;t clump.</p>



<p><strong>Step 2 &ndash; Add the steak:</strong> Use a large, shallow dish (ideally one with a tight-fitting lid) that&rsquo;s big enough to hold your flank steak in a single layer so it can lie flat and be fully coated in the marinade.&nbsp;</p>



<p>If you&rsquo;re working with a larger cut or want less cleanup, a resealable plastic bag will work as well. Pour the marinade over the top, making sure the entire surface is covered.</p>



<p>I like to press the steak gently into the marinade to help it really soak in. If you&rsquo;re using a plastic bag, seal it tightly and massage the marinade into the meat to distribute the liquid evenly.&nbsp;</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Flank-steak-seasoning.jpg" alt="Marinated flank steak before and after it is grilled." class="wp-image-82800" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Flank-steak-seasoning.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Flank-steak-seasoning-350x217.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Flank-steak-seasoning-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Flank-steak-seasoning-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Flank-steak-seasoning-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Flank-steak-seasoning.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 3 &ndash; Cover and marinate:</strong> Cover your dish with a tight-fitting lid (or seal your bag). If you can plan ahead, marinating overnight in the fridge is my go-to, but even a quick 1-hour marinade will still give you good flavor. Depending on your schedule, here&rsquo;s how I do both:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>1 Hour Marinade:</strong> If all you have is an hour, you can let it sit at room temperature on your kitchen counter, which helps take the chill off the meat, allowing it to cook more evenly later.&nbsp;</li>



<li><strong>Overnight Marinade (preferred method):</strong> If you can plan ahead, marinating it overnight in the fridge is even better. Just don&rsquo;t go past 24 hours; the acids in the marinade will start to change the texture, and instead of tender, the meat can turn a little mushy.</li>
</ul>
</div>



